<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  GENERATED by tools/generate_description.py. Do not edit by hand.

  Links, masses and inertia tensors are transcribed from the SolidWorks
  export. The suspension's four closed loops, which URDF cannot express,
  are replaced by linear joint couplings derived in tools/derive_ratios.py
  and held at runtime by rocker_coupling_node, which applies them as
  torques, since Gazebo has no solver-level way to enforce them:

      BLS_joint = -0.57716 x FLS_joint
      BRS_joint = +0.57716 x FLS_joint
      FRS_joint = -1.00774 x FLS_joint

  leaving FLS_joint as the single free suspension coordinate.

  Joint names carry a _joint suffix because SDF keeps links and joints in
  one namespace and the export gave them matching names. Link and frame
  names are unchanged from the CAD.
-->
